From a83a354ca7799bc933612ab1bb16c8f93db3b72a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:14:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 080/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: bound the slice segment
 index

hevc_frame::cur_slice_idx indexes ref_poc_list[2][MAX_SLICE_NUM][16] in
eight places, and codec_hevc_process_segment() increments it once per
continuation slice with nothing bounding it. The bitstream decides how
many slice segments arrive before one restarts at segment address 0, so a
corrupt, truncated or hostile stream drives it without end.

The overrun is quiet at first and then destructive. ref_poc_list[0] is
followed in memory by ref_poc_list[1], so indices 800..1599 merely alias
the second list and corrupt reference POCs. Past 1599 the writes leave the
~100KB hevc_frame allocation entirely, and codec_hevc_update_frame_refs()
zeroes 16 words per slice into whatever shares the slab.

Seen on a 4K HEVC Main10 stream after a long run of

  meson-vdec: Couldn't find ref. frame 63

which is the same stream damage that stops the segments restarting:

  Unable to handle kernel read from unreadable memory at virtual address 0000000000000030
  pc : codec_hevc_process_segment+0x1fc/0x1008 [meson_vdec]
  Call trace:
   codec_hevc_process_segment+0x1fc/0x1008 [meson_vdec]
   codec_hevc_threaded_isr+0x1d0/0x230 [meson_vdec]
   vdec_threaded_isr+0x1c/0x24 [meson_vdec]

0x30 is hevc_frame::cur_slice_type and the faulting instruction is the
first dereference in codec_hevc_set_mc(), reached with hevc->cur_frame
NULL - reloaded from memory immediately after codec_hevc_fill_mmu_map(),
one line before it is used. No writer of cur_frame can run at that point:
the threaded ISR holds hevc->lock and every assignment of the field takes
it. The field was corrupted, not assigned.

Bound the counter and drop the frame when it is reached; decoding resumes
at the next frame start, which is what the stall recovery already does for
frames it abandons. Also refuse to dereference a NULL cur_frame in the
segment tail, so a corrupted field is a warning and a failed segment
rather than an oops.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
---
 drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c | 35 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c
index 28f8b0268080..b157c52e654a 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c
@@ -1629,6 +1629,28 @@ static int codec_hevc_process_segment(struct amvdec_session *sess)
 		 */
 		if (!hevc->cur_frame)
 			return codec_hevc_skip_slice(sess);
+
+		/*
+		 * ref_poc_list[] holds MAX_SLICE_NUM slice segments and
+		 * nothing in the bitstream bounds this counter.  A stream
+		 * whose segments never restart at address 0 - corrupt,
+		 * truncated, or hostile - increments it without end, and
+		 * every ref_poc_list[0][cur_slice_idx][] write then walks
+		 * off that list: first aliasing ref_poc_list[1], then
+		 * spraying zeroes past the end of the 100KB hevc_frame
+		 * allocation and into whatever shares the slab.
+		 *
+		 * Drop the frame instead.  Playback resumes at the next
+		 * frame start, which is what the stall recovery would have
+		 * done anyway.
+		 */
+		if (hevc->cur_frame->cur_slice_idx + 1 >= MAX_SLICE_NUM) {
+			dev_warn_ratelimited(core->dev,
+					     "%u slice segments without a frame start; dropping frame\n",
+					     MAX_SLICE_NUM);
+			return codec_hevc_skip_slice(sess);
+		}
+
 		hevc->cur_frame->cur_slice_idx++;
 	}
 
@@ -1640,6 +1662,19 @@ static int codec_hevc_process_segment(struct amvdec_session *sess)
 		codec_hevc_fill_mmu_map(sess, &hevc->common,
 					&hevc->cur_frame->vbuf->vb2_buf,
 					hevc->is_10bit);
+	/*
+	 * Everything above reads hevc->cur_frame through hevc, so the
+	 * compiler must reload it after each call - and an oops has been
+	 * seen where the reload following codec_hevc_fill_mmu_map()
+	 * returned NULL, dereferenced one line later in
+	 * codec_hevc_set_mc().  No writer of cur_frame can run here (the
+	 * threaded ISR holds hevc->lock and every writer takes it), so a
+	 * NULL at this point means the field was corrupted rather than
+	 * assigned.  Fail the segment loudly instead of oopsing.
+	 */
+	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(!hevc->cur_frame))
+		return -1;
+
 	codec_hevc_set_mc(sess, hevc->cur_frame);
 	codec_hevc_set_mcrcc(sess);
 	codec_hevc_set_mpred(sess, hevc->cur_frame, hevc->col_frame);
